1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
From the bleakness of a vast desert

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:07,040
arose a one-of-a-kind metropolis,

3
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:11,140
a neon-filled paradise built by the whims of culture

4
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:14,040
and the dictates of commerce.

5
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:16,720
For 50 years, Las Vegas has taken visitors

6
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:19,580
to the edge of their imaginations,

7
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:24,620
transporting them to medieval England, decadent Rome,

8
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:30,000
ancient Egypt, and bustling New York City.

9
00:00:30,001 --> 00:00:34,240
Within one medium-sized city stands 11 of the world's 12

10
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:41,040
largest hotels, gateways to the biggest playground on Earth.

11
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:45,540
Coming up on modern marvels, Las Vegas hotels,

12
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:46,760
desert fantasy.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,580
In Las Vegas, form follows fantasy.

14
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:10,540
And in all of these fantasy environments,

15
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:14,880
you find ideas, emotions that tap in.

16
00:01:14,980 --> 00:01:18,480
And you find ideas that tap into the world.

17
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:21,460
And you find ideas that tap into the world.

18
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:27,460
And you find ideas that tap into the world.

19
00:01:27,461 --> 00:01:32,460
emotions that tap into some very deep feelings

20
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:34,060
of the mass market.

21
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:37,800
There's a desire for a sense of meaning, of narrative,

22
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:41,500
being able to put yourself into these different stories,

23
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:45,820
whether it's pirates or ancient Egypt or South Sea

24
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:49,520
Islands or the Old West.

25
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:52,520
The storytellers behind Las Vegas hotels

26
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:57,460
have proved the truth of Build It and They Will Come.

27
00:01:57,461 --> 00:02:04,560
Between 1990 and 1996, the city added 33,000 new hotel rooms,

28
00:02:04,561 --> 00:02:06,560
and an equal number of new visitors

29
00:02:06,561 --> 00:02:09,600
flocked to occupy them.

30
00:02:09,601 --> 00:02:13,380
What other city in the world, from the moment you arrive,

31
00:02:13,381 --> 00:02:15,380
do you become part of a fantasy?

32
00:02:15,381 --> 00:02:20,960
We're the world's largest adult theme park, where you recognize

33
00:02:20,961 --> 00:02:24,680
you're going to come, and for three to five days,

34
00:02:24,681 --> 00:02:29,680
You can be anywhere you want to be and anyone you choose to be.

35
00:02:29,681 --> 00:02:32,680
Pirates are not in contraband on the dock!

36
00:02:32,681 --> 00:02:37,680
The scurvy cutthroats and the cheap that fly the Jolly Roger!

37
00:02:37,681 --> 00:02:40,680
These buildings are not monuments.

38
00:02:40,681 --> 00:02:45,680
They sometimes are getting perceived as monuments, but they're not.

39
00:02:45,681 --> 00:02:48,680
We're going to change them. We are going to change these buildings.

40
00:02:48,681 --> 00:02:52,680
I mean, I have been doing this for 20 years, and even I have torn down things

41
00:02:52,681 --> 00:02:55,980
that I have built in that short of a career.

42
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:16,880
The fantasy that never stops began life as a way station

43
00:03:16,980 --> 00:03:20,280
in the timeless Nevada desert.

44
00:03:20,281 --> 00:03:23,380
Located on the rail line between Los Angeles and Salt Lake

45
00:03:23,480 --> 00:03:27,980
City, Las Vegas was established in 1903

46
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:30,220
as a sober, working-class community

47
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:33,420
to support the railroad.

48
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:35,820
The tiny hamlet went about its quiet ways

49
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:40,140
until government actions transformed the area.

50
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:44,440
In 1931, gambling was legalized.

51
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:48,280
The same year, construction began on the Boulder Dam,

52
00:03:48,281 --> 00:03:52,780
just 30 miles from the city limits.

53
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:55,380
The dam, later renamed for Herbert Hoover,

54
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:58,460
flooded the area with thousands of workers

55
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:04,560
eager for a place to spend their paychecks on Saturday nights.

56
00:04:04,660 --> 00:04:08,260
The population expanded because of so many thousands

57
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:10,040
of workers working on the dam.

58
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:13,700
But also, Las Vegas have never seen itself as a resort

59
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:16,480
community, as so many hundreds of thousands of people

60
00:04:16,481 --> 00:04:19,320
to see this huge, magnificent project going up.

61
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:22,780
This was the time when Las Vegas first began to see itself

62
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:24,960
as possibly a resort destination.

63
00:04:25,060 --> 00:04:26,920
So the dam played a very crucial role in that.

64
00:04:30,420 --> 00:04:33,500
In the early years, what Las Vegas thought it had to sell

65
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:36,360
was the Old West.

66
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:39,000
The mythical American West featured saloons

67
00:04:39,100 --> 00:04:41,000
and gambling halls.

68
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:43,900
And in Vegas, one could find something close to the real

69
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,480
thing.

70
00:04:46,481 --> 00:04:49,980
You could drink and carouse like an old-time cowboy,

71
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:53,320
enjoying authentic liquor and a campy, tongue-in-cheek

72
00:04:53,420 --> 00:04:56,020
atmosphere.

73
00:04:56,120 --> 00:05:00,020
The big breakthrough came in 1941 with the opening

74
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:03,240
of the El Rancho Hotel.

75
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:06,040
A rambling ranch house, it transplanted

76
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:10,240
the imagined splendor of the Old West to casinos

77
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:13,140
and brought lavish decor and thematic architecture

78
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:13,940
to the strip.

79
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:21,060
A year later, the last frontier developed a couple of miles

80
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:22,720
further out the strip.

81
00:05:22,820 --> 00:05:24,860
Old timers at the time were shaking their heads

82
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:27,220
and saying Las Vegas is simply not large enough

83
00:05:27,320 --> 00:05:29,220
to accommodate two resort hotels.

84
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:36,300
But Vegas and its Western theme flourished.

85
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:39,740
Over the next decade, gambling houses with evocative names

86
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:44,580
like Hotel Apache and the Golden Nugget sprang up

87
00:05:44,581 --> 00:05:46,280
up across the desert landscape.

88
00:05:49,320 --> 00:05:52,880
In 1951, the casino Pioneer Club

89
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:57,820
introduced a neon icon named Vegas Vic.

90
00:05:57,920 --> 00:06:02,740
His arm was animated, and he had a cigarette in his mouth

91
00:06:02,840 --> 00:06:03,840
that kept flickering.

92
00:06:03,940 --> 00:06:08,040
And originally, as well, his voice said, howdy, partner.

93
00:06:08,140 --> 00:06:12,480
A friendly, lanky, cowboy type, which became,

94
00:06:12,481 --> 00:06:16,020
from the early 1950s on, real logo of Las Vegas.

95
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:21,820
While the Western theme sold well,

96
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:24,320
California investor Billy Wilkerson

97
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:27,460
dreamed of attracting a more sophisticated clientele

98
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:30,400
to the desert.

99
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:35,500
In 1945, he began construction on the Flamingo Hotel,

100
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:39,400
only to run out of money before completing it.

101
00:06:39,401 --> 00:06:42,440
Mobster Bugsy Siegel stepped into the breach,

102
00:06:42,540 --> 00:06:45,600
buying the Flamingo and sparing no expense

103
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:49,640
in making it the most modern, most luxurious destination

104
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:52,640
in Vegas history.

105
00:06:52,740 --> 00:06:55,240
Here was a swank Hollywood nightclub

106
00:06:55,340 --> 00:06:57,460
transported to the Nevada desert.

107
00:07:00,260 --> 00:07:03,360
The Flamingo had a special attraction.

108
00:07:03,460 --> 00:07:05,460
The Flamingo had a showroom.

109
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:08,600
And it brought some serious name entertainment

110
00:07:08,601 --> 00:07:11,000
to Las Vegas.

111
00:07:11,100 --> 00:07:13,640
They became an attraction unto themselves.

112
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:15,900
People came for the entertainment.

113
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,840
I remember my first show that I ever saw in a nightclub

114
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:24,980
was at the Flamingo, and I saw Lena Horne.

115
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:26,580
And I was absolutely astounded.

116
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:32,660
Despite its star power, the Flamingo

117
00:07:32,760 --> 00:07:36,300
at first floundered under its then unheard of $6 million

118
00:07:36,301 --> 00:07:40,000
construction costs.

119
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:42,940
His mob patrons blame Bugsy for the slow return

120
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:45,340
on their investment.

121
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:48,040
Just seven months after his hotel opened,

122
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:50,140
he was gunned down in his Hollywood home.

123
00:07:56,060 --> 00:07:59,900
Bugsy was singularly unsuccessful until he died.

124
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,400
And I think people came to Las Vegas

125
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:06,300
to see what this guy created, what it got to be.

126
00:08:06,301 --> 00:08:10,440
in so much trouble that his business associates

127
00:08:10,540 --> 00:08:11,340
had eliminated him.

128
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:17,740
The Flamingo's first owner had gone bust.

129
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:21,080
Its second had spent other people's money with abandon

130
00:08:21,180 --> 00:08:24,520
and paid the price.

131
00:08:24,620 --> 00:08:27,820
Yet in time, their joint vision was vindicated

132
00:08:27,920 --> 00:08:29,960
as the Flamingo became a moneymaker

133
00:08:30,060 --> 00:08:34,720
and established a whole new style for Las Vegas.

134
00:08:34,721 --> 00:08:38,420
Attracted by plush surroundings and top entertainment,

135
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:41,620
men in tuxedos and women in evening gowns

136
00:08:41,720 --> 00:08:43,920
came to gamble away their riches.

137
00:08:46,460 --> 00:08:49,560
The sophistication of the Flamingo

138
00:08:49,660 --> 00:08:52,980
just broke open the market for Las Vegas.

139
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:57,080
It was no longer just this Western Dude Ranch,

140
00:08:57,180 --> 00:08:58,780
easygoing sort of place.

141
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:00,220
It upped the ante.

142
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:03,820
It was a much more glamorous and sophisticated place.

143
00:09:04,720 --> 00:09:07,860
Bold entrepreneurs and timely government projects

144
00:09:07,960 --> 00:09:11,900
had transformed the desert into an oasis.

145
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,840
The glitter of ancient Rome would soon invite the wonderland

146
00:09:15,940 --> 00:09:19,800
to new heights of hedonism.

147
00:09:28,940 --> 00:09:31,220
With the success of the Flamingo Hotel,

148
00:09:31,221 --> 00:09:35,220
Las Vegas entered a golden age of expansion.

149
00:09:35,320 --> 00:09:39,620
Self-contained casino resorts sprang up as fast as they

150
00:09:39,720 --> 00:09:41,960
could be built.

151
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:46,700
By the mid-1950s, Highway 91 in the middle of Nevada

152
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:51,180
had become a glittering three-mile tribute to hedonism.

153
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:56,640
What followed the Flamingo, one after another,

154
00:09:56,740 --> 00:10:01,220
were bigger and more hotels.

155
00:10:01,221 --> 00:10:03,020
more of everything.

156
00:10:03,120 --> 00:10:05,460
And they were all little mini oasises.

157
00:10:08,760 --> 00:10:12,600
Each new pleasure palace was determined to top the rest.

158
00:10:12,700 --> 00:10:15,800
In 1953, the Flamingo was forced to remodel

159
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:19,940
to compete with the ultra modern Sands Hotel.

160
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:23,740
The casinos vied to attract top entertainers.

161
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:26,880
And gambling revenue allowed them to lure talent like Frank

162
00:10:26,980 --> 00:10:31,220
Sinatra with paychecks of $50,000 a week.

163
00:10:31,221 --> 00:10:37,720
One of the biggest draws was a series of atomic bomb tests

164
00:10:37,820 --> 00:10:41,860
conducted over the Las Vegas proving grounds.

165
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:48,740
Las Vegas learned how to use the nuclear bomb for publicity.

166
00:10:48,840 --> 00:10:53,080
They would have things like Miss Atomic Bomb contests.

167
00:10:53,180 --> 00:10:55,640
They would have mushroom hairdos.

168
00:10:55,740 --> 00:10:59,520
There was a contest for atomic cocktails.

169
00:10:59,521 --> 00:11:04,800
cocktails, and even the hotels on the then emerging Las Vegas

170
00:11:04,900 --> 00:11:09,100
Strip would publish schedules of nuclear tests,

171
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:12,300
would invite their guests to take box lunches up

172
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:13,760
to the mountains.

173
00:11:13,860 --> 00:11:17,000
And in the pre-dawn hours, they would be able to see the sky

174
00:11:17,100 --> 00:11:19,940
light up and those pretty mushroom clouds float away.

175
00:11:25,440 --> 00:11:28,040
The entertainers, the atomic picnics,

176
00:11:28,041 --> 00:11:31,840
the resort amenities, all were merely devices

177
00:11:31,940 --> 00:11:35,780
to draw customers into casinos.

178
00:11:35,880 --> 00:11:39,920
Inside, then as now, there were no clocks, windows,

179
00:11:40,020 --> 00:11:44,920
or easily found exits, creating an air of unreality

180
00:11:45,020 --> 00:11:48,920
that treated gamblers like bouncing pinballs.

181
00:11:49,020 --> 00:11:51,660
There tended to not be straight axes through casinos.

182
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:54,040
You kind of had to wind your way through.

183
00:11:54,140 --> 00:11:56,060
You didn't know what was left, right,

184
00:11:56,061 --> 00:11:57,860
He barely knew what was up and down,

185
00:11:57,960 --> 00:12:00,100
because you could use mirrors every place to confuse you

186
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:01,160
with that one.

187
00:12:01,260 --> 00:12:03,600
And you certainly didn't know whether it was night or day.

188
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:12,680
In the 1950s and 60s, Las Vegas was a completely car

189
00:12:12,780 --> 00:12:15,240
oriented environment.

190
00:12:15,340 --> 00:12:17,680
One could drive the entire strip without spotting

191
00:12:17,780 --> 00:12:19,020
a pedestrian.

192
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:22,580
And with nary a sidewalk, cars were used even to travel

193
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:23,760
to a neighboring building.

194
00:12:26,060 --> 00:12:29,660
Hotel architecture adjusted to accommodate the car,

195
00:12:29,760 --> 00:12:32,400
providing ever flashier signs designed

196
00:12:32,500 --> 00:12:33,800
to be read from the highway.

197
00:12:37,800 --> 00:12:41,400
In Las Vegas, they took the neon sign

198
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:45,020
and they made it something completely unique, completely

199
00:12:45,120 --> 00:12:46,020
new.

200
00:12:46,120 --> 00:12:50,060
It was this image of the neon sign,

201
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:52,720
which told you everything you needed to know about the building

202
00:12:52,820 --> 00:12:54,320
and why you should be there.

203
00:12:54,321 --> 00:12:55,560
It drew people in.

204
00:13:07,060 --> 00:13:12,080
Built in 1958, the Stardust sign was three stories high,

205
00:13:12,180 --> 00:13:16,080
with lights bursting into a cosmic explosion that

206
00:13:16,180 --> 00:13:19,320
progressed through a 20-minute animation sequence.

207
00:13:19,420 --> 00:13:22,180
A purely decorative piece of architecture,

208
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:23,720
totally made out of light.

209
00:13:23,721 --> 00:13:25,960
The whole building created the illusion

210
00:13:26,060 --> 00:13:28,900
that it was just one big light show.

211
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,800
It was like the modern kind of computerized extravaganzas

212
00:13:32,900 --> 00:13:36,760
and LED signs we see today at places like Times Square,

213
00:13:36,860 --> 00:13:38,160
way before it's time.

214
00:13:50,880 --> 00:13:53,720
In Las Vegas of the 1960s,

215
00:13:53,721 --> 00:13:58,720
builder Jay Sarno stood out as a showman and a visionary.

216
00:13:58,820 --> 00:14:01,460
He took the notion of thematic architecture

217
00:14:01,560 --> 00:14:06,800
and raised it to a new level with Caesar's Palace.

218
00:14:06,900 --> 00:14:10,940
In 1966, when Caesar's Palace opened,

219
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:13,340
they brought a special kind of magic.

220
00:14:13,440 --> 00:14:17,340
Caesar's had a very, very special quality to it.

221
00:14:17,440 --> 00:14:20,580
It had a theme, a real honest to God theme.

222
00:14:20,581 --> 00:14:27,380
Caesar's was actually a hodgepodge of Roman, Greek, Baroque,

223
00:14:27,480 --> 00:14:29,860
and even modern architecture.

224
00:14:29,960 --> 00:14:33,920
The building presented no particular period in time.

225
00:14:34,020 --> 00:14:37,560
It represented a mystique of casual elegance

226
00:14:37,660 --> 00:14:42,100
that has only grown over its 30 wildly successful years.

227
00:14:44,280 --> 00:14:47,600
From the swimming pool shaped like a Roman bath

228
00:14:47,601 --> 00:14:51,600
to the heroic statues strewn about the casino.

229
00:14:51,700 --> 00:14:54,840
This was the most completely themed casino

230
00:14:54,940 --> 00:14:56,840
Las Vegas had ever seen.

231
00:15:04,960 --> 00:15:08,160
Good design, good architecture, whatever it might be,

232
00:15:08,260 --> 00:15:09,360
is consistent.

233
00:15:09,460 --> 00:15:13,800
The more consistent it is, the easier it is to understand.

234
00:15:13,900 --> 00:15:17,600
You don't want the experience to be a cerebral experience.

235
00:15:17,601 --> 00:15:19,100
You want it just to happen.

236
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:20,740
You don't want anybody to think about it.

237
00:15:20,840 --> 00:15:24,400
You want them just to enjoy it.

238
00:15:24,500 --> 00:15:26,840
From the moment they walked in the door,

239
00:15:26,940 --> 00:15:30,640
guests at Caesars enjoyed the feeling of being special.

240
00:15:30,740 --> 00:15:34,480
And they happily paid a premium for the experience.

241
00:15:34,580 --> 00:15:37,660
Caesar's Palace, there's no apostrophe in there.

242
00:15:37,760 --> 00:15:39,520
It's plural, Caesars.

243
00:15:39,620 --> 00:15:43,660
And Sarno explained this as everyone who comes there

244
00:15:43,760 --> 00:15:44,960
is a Caesar.

245
00:15:44,961 --> 00:15:48,960
He wasn't talking about any one Caesar who had this palace.

246
00:15:49,060 --> 00:15:52,960
Any visitor, any gambler who came to Caesar's palace

247
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:54,600
was the Caesar.

248
00:15:54,700 --> 00:15:56,700
This was theme architecture.

249
00:15:56,800 --> 00:15:59,700
It was creating an entire environment which

250
00:15:59,800 --> 00:16:03,720
would transport people to another time, another place.

251
00:16:06,920 --> 00:16:09,820
While Sarno was busy constructing Caesar's,

252
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:14,960
another maverick came to town, billionaire Howard Hughes.

253
00:16:14,961 --> 00:16:19,560
Hughes had a mission, to rid Las Vegas of its shady, underworld

254
00:16:19,660 --> 00:16:21,460
aura.

255
00:16:21,560 --> 00:16:26,100
From 1965 to 1970, he lived in the penthouse floor

256
00:16:26,200 --> 00:16:30,200
of the Desert Inn, going so far as to buy the hotel when they

257
00:16:30,300 --> 00:16:34,620
threatened to evict their eccentric tenant.

258
00:16:34,720 --> 00:16:37,120
Without ever emerging from his room,

259
00:16:37,220 --> 00:16:39,320
Hughes bought a series of casinos

260
00:16:39,420 --> 00:16:44,960
and threw his support behind the Corporate Gaming Act of 1969.

261
00:16:44,961 --> 00:16:49,960
Prior to 1969, all owners of a hotel had to be licensed,

262
00:16:50,060 --> 00:16:51,540
and that would have been impossible

263
00:16:51,640 --> 00:16:53,400
with a broadly held company.

264
00:16:53,500 --> 00:16:56,680
After 1969, of course, corporations like Holiday

265
00:16:56,780 --> 00:17:01,240
Inn, MGM, Bally's, and so forth could provide enormous sums

266
00:17:01,340 --> 00:17:03,680
of money for the future development of Las Vegas.

267
00:17:05,620 --> 00:17:08,320
Fueled by deep corporate pockets,

268
00:17:08,420 --> 00:17:12,260
Vegas turned to mega hotels, huge structures

269
00:17:12,261 --> 00:17:14,920
with thousands of rooms.

270
00:17:15,020 --> 00:17:18,100
These hotels were among the largest in the world.

271
00:17:18,200 --> 00:17:21,500
Their use as casinos only added to the difficulty

272
00:17:21,600 --> 00:17:23,000
of designing them.

273
00:17:24,900 --> 00:17:27,800
They're buildings that operate 24 hours a day,

274
00:17:27,900 --> 00:17:29,720
seven days a week.

275
00:17:29,820 --> 00:17:34,520
That brings a whole nother multitude of problems

276
00:17:34,620 --> 00:17:38,160
that need to be dealt with in a facility that never sleeps,

277
00:17:38,260 --> 00:17:40,320
it never closes down.

278
00:17:40,321 --> 00:17:43,660
unique design problems for the 21st century.

279
00:17:45,760 --> 00:17:48,060
Caesar's impresario, Jay Sarno,

280
00:17:48,160 --> 00:17:52,640
had shown the limitless potential of hotel theming.

281
00:17:52,740 --> 00:17:56,000
Howard Hughes had cleared the way for corporate America

282
00:17:56,100 --> 00:17:58,140
to invade the world of gambling.

283
00:18:00,400 --> 00:18:04,540
Backed by imagination and money, Vegas hotels

284
00:18:04,640 --> 00:18:07,120
were poised to reach for the sky.

285
00:18:10,320 --> 00:18:24,220
Howard Hughes left Las Vegas for good in 1970, leaving behind a legacy of corporate money.

286
00:18:24,900 --> 00:18:31,920
In his wake, international hotel chains moved in, erecting larger versions of their typical

287
00:18:31,921 --> 00:18:39,360
buildings. It changed the atmosphere of Las Vegas. You didn't have these individual,

288
00:18:39,361 --> 00:18:42,960
entrepreneurs with a vision who were able to force through

289
00:18:43,060 --> 00:18:46,260
something like Caesar's Palace, who were able to force through

290
00:18:46,360 --> 00:18:50,500
by their own will something like the Flamingo Hotel.

291
00:18:53,100 --> 00:18:57,020
High-rises began dominating the strip, dwarfing and replacing

292
00:18:57,120 --> 00:19:01,720
the great neon signs that had defined Las Vegas.

293
00:19:01,820 --> 00:19:06,160
A single hotel casino might service 3,000 guests, along

294
00:19:06,161 --> 00:19:08,960
along with nearly as many support staff,

295
00:19:09,060 --> 00:19:13,600
demanding a vast infrastructure.

296
00:19:13,700 --> 00:19:15,140
You have to take into consideration

297
00:19:15,240 --> 00:19:19,040
the fact that there has to be a huge amount of redundancy.

298
00:19:19,140 --> 00:19:21,300
So you never rely on one water source.

299
00:19:21,400 --> 00:19:24,020
You never rely on one power source.

300
00:19:24,120 --> 00:19:27,540
Things that are at the heart, the energy that the building

301
00:19:27,640 --> 00:19:30,220
requires for its physical demands

302
00:19:30,320 --> 00:19:33,260
are engineered in a completely different way

303
00:19:33,261 --> 00:19:36,660
because of this factor of never closing the building down.

304
00:19:36,760 --> 00:19:42,700
I mean, that is the mortal sin, never to close the building.

305
00:19:42,800 --> 00:19:46,200
Las Vegas' new corporate master soon realized

306
00:19:46,300 --> 00:19:51,280
that size meant little without a storyline behind it.

307
00:19:51,380 --> 00:19:54,780
The way to a customer's heart and his wallet

308
00:19:54,880 --> 00:19:57,980
was to create the best costume party around

309
00:19:58,080 --> 00:20:01,780
and ask him to join the fun.

310
00:20:01,781 --> 00:20:04,680
What we do in Las Vegas is that we put you on stage.

311
00:20:04,780 --> 00:20:07,320
We invite you to come onto our stage.

312
00:20:07,420 --> 00:20:11,620
What we'd like to inject into the whole thing is romance.

313
00:20:11,720 --> 00:20:14,920
The way romance makes us feel, we never get tired of.

314
00:20:15,020 --> 00:20:18,220
So if we can incorporate romance into something,

315
00:20:18,320 --> 00:20:21,260
then I will have you back and back many, many times

316
00:20:21,360 --> 00:20:25,740
and enjoying yourself and feeling wonderful about yourself.

317
00:20:25,840 --> 00:20:28,540
For the last 20 years, Las Vegas architects

318
00:20:28,541 --> 00:20:32,340
architects have faced a daunting challenge,

319
00:20:32,440 --> 00:20:35,380
to make their hotels bigger than the rest,

320
00:20:35,480 --> 00:20:38,980
to imbue them with a consistent and involving theme,

321
00:20:39,080 --> 00:20:42,380
and to build them fast.

322
00:20:42,480 --> 00:20:45,460
With so much money at stake and constant pressure

323
00:20:45,560 --> 00:20:49,160
to relieve a fickle public of their hard-earned dollars,

324
00:20:49,260 --> 00:20:53,260
these steel giants leap skyward with astonishing speed.

325
00:20:58,540 --> 00:21:03,540
If we were to wait until we actually had the end product

326
00:21:04,540 --> 00:21:08,540
designed, these buildings would be taking four and five

327
00:21:08,541 --> 00:21:12,540
years to build instead of two and three years to build.

328
00:21:12,541 --> 00:21:15,540
So what we've had to do is we've had to go after a more

329
00:21:15,541 --> 00:21:18,540
universal structure.

330
00:21:18,541 --> 00:21:21,540
By universal structures, I mean spaces that have far less

331
00:21:21,541 --> 00:21:25,540
columns in them, buildings that have much higher ceilings in

332
00:21:25,541 --> 00:21:31,580
higher ceilings in them, which give designers much more flexibility as to how they're going

333
00:21:31,581 --> 00:21:38,800
to arrange the non-structural or non-bearing walls inside the movie set.

334
00:21:38,801 --> 00:21:40,920
I mean, that's what these are.

335
00:21:40,921 --> 00:21:44,100
These are not historical buildings.

336
00:21:44,101 --> 00:21:49,220
People will not be coming here 2,000 years from now and looking at these buildings.

337
00:21:49,221 --> 00:21:54,480
This is one large movie set that continually evolves and continually changes.

338
00:21:54,481 --> 00:22:00,980
Suddenly, in 1978, Las Vegas faced its worst nightmare

339
00:22:01,080 --> 00:22:05,220
when Atlantic City, New Jersey legalized gambling.

340
00:22:05,320 --> 00:22:08,420
In the years since, gambling has spread to scores

341
00:22:08,520 --> 00:22:12,900
of reservations, riverboats, and localities.

342
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:16,060
Contrary to original fears, the competition

343
00:22:16,160 --> 00:22:19,900
has actually benefited Las Vegas.

344
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:24,340
As it has been, it's been a long time

345
00:22:24,341 --> 00:22:27,840
over the years, Las Vegas has become the place to come.

346
00:22:27,940 --> 00:22:29,640
It's the Mecca for gamblers.

347
00:22:29,740 --> 00:22:31,980
There are so many other things going on around the country,

348
00:22:32,080 --> 00:22:34,980
but basically all that does is whet the appetites

349
00:22:35,080 --> 00:22:38,580
for people to come to the Mecca.

350
00:22:38,680 --> 00:22:42,200
The original MGM Grand, opened in 1972,

351
00:22:42,300 --> 00:22:46,100
was both quasi-film set and brassy evocation

352
00:22:46,200 --> 00:22:50,300
of 1920s movie palaces.

353
00:22:50,400 --> 00:22:53,040
Presenting grandeur with a populous flair,

354
00:22:53,041 --> 00:22:55,980
it offered children the magic of the movies

355
00:22:56,080 --> 00:22:58,980
and adults the nostalgia of a bygone era.

356
00:23:02,680 --> 00:23:06,280
Anticipating huge crowds, the NGM architects

357
00:23:06,380 --> 00:23:09,480
turned a dilemma into an opportunity,

358
00:23:09,580 --> 00:23:13,600
designing a massive port cochere, an outdoor awning

359
00:23:13,700 --> 00:23:18,540
capable of covering scores of limousines.

360
00:23:18,640 --> 00:23:21,200
At once decorative and functional,

361
00:23:21,201 --> 00:23:25,440
The port cocher created the Grand Entrance,

362
00:23:25,540 --> 00:23:31,780
an elegant ceremony presided over by costumed doormen.

363
00:23:31,880 --> 00:23:33,080
This started a trend.

364
00:23:33,180 --> 00:23:36,720
You'll see in a succession of hotels after that,

365
00:23:36,820 --> 00:23:41,480
each port cocher got larger, more glamorous, more glitzy

366
00:23:41,580 --> 00:23:45,320
throughout the 1970s.

367
00:23:45,420 --> 00:23:49,120
In 1980, tragedy struck when a deadly fire swept

368
00:23:49,121 --> 00:23:54,020
swept through the MGM Grand, killing 84 people.

369
00:23:54,021 --> 00:23:56,960
During the blaze, some gamblers on hot streaks

370
00:23:56,961 --> 00:24:00,640
refused to leave the football field-sized casino

371
00:24:00,641 --> 00:24:04,480
and were finally herded out just minutes before advancing flames

372
00:24:04,481 --> 00:24:06,780
consumed its 1,000 slot machines.

373
00:24:14,200 --> 00:24:16,620
Today, high-tech monitoring systems

374
00:24:16,621 --> 00:24:21,120
safety procedures among the most stringent in the world.

375
00:24:21,220 --> 00:24:22,860
And there is much to monitor.

376
00:24:22,960 --> 00:24:27,160
A lone mega hotel consumes 15 million gallons of water

377
00:24:27,260 --> 00:24:31,460
and 7 million kilowatts of electricity each month,

378
00:24:31,560 --> 00:24:36,280
enough to service over 3,000 homes.

379
00:24:36,380 --> 00:24:40,280
Technology helps also to safeguard the profit margin,

380
00:24:40,380 --> 00:24:43,680
as eye-in-the-sky cameras capture the play at gaming

381
00:24:43,780 --> 00:24:46,620
tables.

382
00:24:46,621 --> 00:24:50,060
Pictures from every table are flashed to a giant control

383
00:24:50,160 --> 00:24:53,160
center where they are examined 24 hours a day.

384
00:24:56,360 --> 00:24:59,060
Anyone on a hot streak gets a closer look

385
00:24:59,160 --> 00:25:01,860
and has his photo preserved for future reference.

386
00:25:16,620 --> 00:25:20,620
With an ever greater concentration of huge hotels,

387
00:25:20,720 --> 00:25:24,620
Las Vegas has gradually become an urban environment.

388
00:25:24,720 --> 00:25:31,620
In the town where Carr was king, people now walk between competing attractions.

389
00:25:31,720 --> 00:25:36,620
The hotels have literally put their front doors right out on the edge of their property

390
00:25:36,720 --> 00:25:41,620
with some kind of big moving sidewalk like into the Mirage or into Caesar's Palace

391
00:25:41,720 --> 00:25:44,620
that scoops you up right at the very edge of the sidewalk

392
00:25:44,621 --> 00:25:48,120
of the sidewalk and whisks you into the casino.

393
00:25:48,220 --> 00:25:50,020
The funny thing about that is, of course,

394
00:25:50,120 --> 00:25:52,860
that those moving sidewalks only work one direction.

395
00:25:52,960 --> 00:25:55,460
It isn't like in an airport where you can take a moving

396
00:25:55,560 --> 00:25:57,760
sidewalk going both directions.

397
00:25:57,860 --> 00:25:59,560
In Vegas, you can only go in.

398
00:25:59,660 --> 00:26:03,180
You're on your own getting out.

399
00:26:03,280 --> 00:26:06,480
Whether on foot or in a car, visitors to the strip

400
00:26:06,580 --> 00:26:09,180
frequently find themselves trapped with mobs

401
00:26:09,280 --> 00:26:12,880
of fellow fun seekers.

402
00:26:12,881 --> 00:26:15,780
Personally, I think gridlock is good.

403
00:26:15,880 --> 00:26:19,680
I think gridlock is part of the reason people come to Las Vegas.

404
00:26:19,780 --> 00:26:23,020
They love to be with other people.

405
00:26:23,120 --> 00:26:28,860
This is Times Square almost every night of the year.

406
00:26:28,960 --> 00:26:31,360
Like Times Square, in recent years,

407
00:26:31,460 --> 00:26:36,100
Las Vegas hotels have decided to clean up their act.

408
00:26:36,200 --> 00:26:39,600
Beginning with Circus Circus in 1975,

409
00:26:39,601 --> 00:26:42,940
they have actively pursued the family market.

410
00:26:43,040 --> 00:26:46,240
Gone are most of the semi-new dancing girls.

411
00:26:46,340 --> 00:26:49,440
In are the arcades and the animal acts.

412
00:26:59,960 --> 00:27:03,600
In the grand tradition of Las Vegas entrepreneurs,

413
00:27:03,700 --> 00:27:07,900
the transition from adult fantasy land to family resort

414
00:27:07,901 --> 00:27:11,700
was spearheaded by one man.

415
00:27:11,800 --> 00:27:14,300
Steve Wynn had a vision that Las Vegas

416
00:27:14,400 --> 00:27:18,840
could be something more than just casinos with hotel rooms.

417
00:27:18,940 --> 00:27:24,340
He showed the industry that they had only just begun to grow.

418
00:27:24,440 --> 00:27:28,160
He was the catalyst that began the building boom

419
00:27:28,260 --> 00:27:32,700
where every casino had a new and bigger and better idea

420
00:27:32,800 --> 00:27:35,560
on how to entertain our tourist population.

421
00:27:37,900 --> 00:27:40,800
We're right there!

422
00:27:40,801 --> 00:27:42,200
We're right there!

423
00:27:42,201 --> 00:27:43,300
Woo!

424
00:27:43,301 --> 00:27:44,300
Yeah!

425
00:28:04,300 --> 00:28:07,900
In 1977, Vegas pioneer Steve Wynn

426
00:28:07,901 --> 00:28:11,700
and bought the old Golden Nugget Casino.

427
00:28:11,800 --> 00:28:14,540
He promptly added its first hotel wing,

428
00:28:14,640 --> 00:28:17,780
threw in a dash of salesmanship, and revived it

429
00:28:17,880 --> 00:28:21,640
as a money-making operation, a quiet beginning to what

430
00:28:21,740 --> 00:28:23,740
became a very loud empire.

431
00:28:31,260 --> 00:28:34,860
Steve Wynn is probably the most demanding client I've ever had.

432
00:28:34,960 --> 00:28:37,460
He's also the best client I ever had.

433
00:28:37,461 --> 00:28:38,860
He's willing to put some money up,

434
00:28:38,861 --> 00:28:43,240
and he supports everyone on the design team.

435
00:28:43,241 --> 00:28:47,260
And he's pushing all the time and always, always looking

436
00:28:47,261 --> 00:28:50,780
for a better way.

437
00:28:50,781 --> 00:28:54,440
To create the Mirage Hotel in the late 1980s,

438
00:28:54,441 --> 00:28:59,280
Wynn raised a then unheard of $700 million.

439
00:28:59,281 --> 00:29:03,280
He was building the most costly hotel in Las Vegas history

440
00:29:03,281 --> 00:29:05,220
on selling a new concept.

441
00:29:08,220 --> 00:29:09,480
With the opening of the Mirage,

442
00:29:09,580 --> 00:29:12,080
Las Vegas took an entirely different direction,

443
00:29:12,180 --> 00:29:13,920
where it wasn't just about gambling.

444
00:29:14,020 --> 00:29:20,540
It was about each hotel being an attraction in and of itself.

445
00:29:20,640 --> 00:29:24,340
When envisioned the Mirage as a South Seas paradise,

446
00:29:24,440 --> 00:29:26,940
an irresistible tropical playground

447
00:29:27,040 --> 00:29:30,640
sprung from the Nevada desert.

448
00:29:30,641 --> 00:29:32,940
During my 16 years with Steve Wynn,

449
00:29:33,040 --> 00:29:37,740
I learned something probably more valuable than anything else.

450
00:29:37,840 --> 00:29:42,080
What people see as they drive by, as they walk by,

451
00:29:42,180 --> 00:29:45,080
as they approach your place, makes a promise.

452
00:29:45,180 --> 00:29:47,160
Make a promise out front.

453
00:29:47,260 --> 00:29:50,000
And then make sure that you not only keep it,

454
00:29:50,100 --> 00:29:53,100
but you keep on keeping it as they move through the place.

455
00:29:53,200 --> 00:29:54,600
No disappointments.

456
00:29:54,700 --> 00:30:00,040
Any surprises should be surprises of, wow, more.

457
00:30:00,640 --> 00:30:03,980
With the Mirage, Wynn promised a complete environmental

458
00:30:04,080 --> 00:30:08,040
experience to dwarf even Caesar's palace.

459
00:30:08,140 --> 00:30:11,080
Rather than simply a pleasant backdrop to gaming,

460
00:30:11,180 --> 00:30:14,380
this thematic design was its own form of entertainment.

461
00:30:20,100 --> 00:30:23,100
Wynn boldly presented his new kind of casino

462
00:30:23,200 --> 00:30:27,240
by building the first major street side attraction in town,

463
00:30:27,340 --> 00:30:29,700
a 40-foot erupting volcano.

464
00:30:30,640 --> 00:30:38,140
The volcano at the Mirage was a challenge that Steve Wynn put on the table, and that

465
00:30:38,141 --> 00:30:44,240
was to say that not even Walt Disney has attempted a volcano yet.

466
00:30:44,241 --> 00:30:45,840
And that was our mandate.

467
00:30:45,841 --> 00:30:54,460
He wanted to see a volcano that, in fact, everyone would believe it was a real volcano.

468
00:30:54,461 --> 00:31:00,600
This volcano had to erupt safely and spectacularly every hour.

469
00:31:00,601 --> 00:31:02,900
Regulating the amount of natural gas

470
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:05,600
and triggering it with an electronic ignition

471
00:31:05,700 --> 00:31:08,700
made the explosion controllable.

472
00:31:08,800 --> 00:31:11,480
Attention to detail included scenting the blast

473
00:31:11,580 --> 00:31:16,280
with a piña colada aroma that was both pleasant and suggestive

474
00:31:16,380 --> 00:31:19,780
of the South Seas.

475
00:31:19,880 --> 00:31:22,180
What was hard was lighting it.

476
00:31:22,280 --> 00:31:25,760
That's what was hard, because you have the light, number one,

477
00:31:25,860 --> 00:31:27,220
from the explosion.

478
00:31:27,320 --> 00:31:30,160
But in between the explosions, the lighting effect

479
00:31:30,161 --> 00:31:32,900
tumbling water and on the stonework.

480
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:35,460
That was by far the most challenging thing.

481
00:31:38,160 --> 00:31:41,740
For much of the Mirage Decor, Wynn and his design team

482
00:31:41,840 --> 00:31:45,780
went with the theory that God is the best architect,

483
00:31:45,880 --> 00:31:49,280
bringing in real tropical landscaping.

484
00:31:49,380 --> 00:31:53,120
Believing also that nature can always be improved upon,

485
00:31:53,220 --> 00:31:58,520
they lit the trees with an artificial yet romantic glow.

486
00:31:58,521 --> 00:32:02,120
When you see these beautiful, growing, living things,

487
00:32:02,220 --> 00:32:07,060
lit so sexy, so subtle, so wonderful,

488
00:32:07,160 --> 00:32:09,760
that's when the imagination begins to happen.

489
00:32:09,860 --> 00:32:15,380
And that's when architecture is at its highest point.

490
00:32:15,480 --> 00:32:18,980
For the Mirage high rise, one of nature's most precious

491
00:32:19,080 --> 00:32:23,880
elements was used to create a glittering facade.

492
00:32:23,980 --> 00:32:27,820
Each of its 3,000 rooms look out through glass containing

493
00:32:27,821 --> 00:32:31,820
containing 24-karat gold.

494
00:32:31,920 --> 00:32:35,020
Real gold glass doesn't turn green when the blue sky

495
00:32:35,120 --> 00:32:37,060
is reflected off of it.

496
00:32:37,160 --> 00:32:39,560
The gold glass is made from copper.

497
00:32:39,660 --> 00:32:43,760
When the sun or the sky is reflected off of it, look green.

498
00:32:43,860 --> 00:32:46,080
We didn't want the building to be green.

499
00:32:46,180 --> 00:32:49,480
We wanted it to be gold.

500
00:32:49,580 --> 00:32:53,420
In 1993, Wynn unveiled a larger-than-life version

501
00:32:53,520 --> 00:32:55,980
of every boy's fantasy.

502
00:32:55,981 --> 00:32:58,420
Hotel and Casino.

503
00:32:58,520 --> 00:33:02,120
Here, the sidewalk attraction was Buccaneer Bay,

504
00:33:02,220 --> 00:33:05,460
a Disney-esque pirate village that the pirates of old would

505
00:33:05,560 --> 00:33:07,760
have built if they could have afforded it.

506
00:33:10,300 --> 00:33:13,560
Every 90 minutes each night, a full-scale theatrical

507
00:33:13,660 --> 00:33:17,600
presentation plays out in the bay.

508
00:33:17,700 --> 00:33:20,740
Audiences find themselves right in the thick of the action

509
00:33:20,840 --> 00:33:23,400
as pirates battle a British frigate.

510
00:33:23,401 --> 00:33:25,900
Will you strike your bloody colors?

511
00:33:26,000 --> 00:33:29,300
When I'm in Davy Jones' locker, not before.

512
00:33:29,400 --> 00:33:30,700
As you wish, sir.

513
00:33:30,800 --> 00:33:31,800
Let go.

514
00:33:41,260 --> 00:33:44,260
To design the spectacular, Wynn imported

515
00:33:44,360 --> 00:33:46,760
a host of new creative talent.

516
00:33:46,860 --> 00:33:49,900
